NAME
    strip - take a feature group off this machine, or put it back

SYNOPSIS
    strip
    strip list
    strip <group> [--no-copy]
    strip restore <group> [--from <mount>] [--online [--unanchored]]

DESCRIPTION
    TOS strips itself: once a machine has done its job for a while, you know
    what it uses, and what it does not use is disk it could have. A feature
    group is a set of base-image files the manifest tags together:

      network   the network stack, its four services, chat, their man pages
      internet  the internet card, remote packages, the online repair
      jbod      disk pooling
      i18n      the language catalogs
      cluster   the cluster setup wizard

    The floor -- what Safe Mode and the emergency terminal need -- is in no
    group and can never be stripped. Everything here is root's.

SUBCOMMANDS
    list             Every group, its size on disk, and whether it is here.
    <group>          Delete the group's files. You are asked to type the
                     word `strip`. Unless SRM holds a copy of every file (a
                     baseline stores only the critical ones), --no-copy must
                     say you know the group comes back only from a TOS disk
                     or the network. Reboot to run without it.
    restore <group>  Put the group back. --from <mount> copies each file from
                     a TOS disk (the install disk, a `deploy`ed drive);
                     --online fetches it from the published release with an
                     internet card. Every file is checked against this
                     machine's manifest digest before it is written, so a
                     disk of another build cannot put back the wrong bytes.
                     --unanchored allows --online on a machine whose manifest
                     is not anchored in its EEPROM; --unverified copies a file
                     the manifest has no digest for.

REMOVED ON PURPOSE IS NOT MISSING
    What was stripped is recorded in /etc/stripped.cfg. `verify` lists those
    files as STRIPPED, not missing; `srm scan` does not call them deleted;
    the online repair does not put them back; `deploy` does not copy them.
    The manifest itself is not changed, so its EEPROM anchor still holds.

SEE ALSO
    srm, verify, deploy
